              "type": "function",
              "function": {
                "name": "PageDescription",
                "arguments": "{\"page_summary\": \"The page contains text about the Nazis and their activities in Alpine regions during World War II, with two images. The first image is a black and white historical photograph showing Nazi officials examining documents or maps. The second image is a color photograph of a dramatic mountain landscape with a silhouetted figure standing on a rocky peak surrounded by clouds and mountains. The text discusses Adolf Hitler's romantic fascination with the Alps, Nazi occupation of Alpine countries, and military operations in mountainous areas during WWII.\", \"keyfact\": \"Austrian-born Adolf Hitler had a lifelong romantic fascination with the Alps and by the 1930s established a home in the Obersalzberg region outside of Berchtesgaden, which he visited frequently until the end of his life.\", \"images\": [{\"image_summary\":\"A black and white historical photograph showing Nazi officials in military uniforms examining documents, maps, or plans on a table. Multiple figures are bent over studying the materials intently.\",\"keyfact\":\"The Nazis held looted art in salt mines at Altaussee, such as the Early Netherlandish Ghent Altarpiece, which sustained significant damage.\"},{\"image_summary\":\"A color photograph of a dramatic Alpine landscape featuring a silhouetted figure standing on a rocky mountain peak. The scene shows multiple mountain peaks shrouded in clouds and mist, creating a romantic and imposing atmosphere.\",\"keyfact\":\"The image is credited to Caspar David Friedrich and depicts the romantic Alpine scenery that fascinated Hitler throughout his life.\"}]}"
              }
            }
          ],
          "custom_content": {
            "state": {
              "claude_message_content": [
                {
                  "id": "toolu_bdrk_01E14GiH1JLCNupbmG3JWbrk",
                  "input": {
                    "page_summary": "The page contains text about the Nazis and their activities in Alpine regions during World War II, with two images. The first image is a black and white historical photograph showing Nazi officials examining documents or maps. The second image is a color photograph of a dramatic mountain landscape with a silhouetted figure standing on a rocky peak surrounded by clouds and mountains. The text discusses Adolf Hitler's romantic fascination with the Alps, Nazi occupation of Alpine countries, and military operations in mountainous areas during WWII.",
                    "keyfact": "Austrian-born Adolf Hitler had a lifelong romantic fascination with the Alps and by the 1930s established a home in the Obersalzberg region outside of Berchtesgaden, which he visited frequently until the end of his life.",
                    "images": [
                      {
                        "image_summary": "A black and white historical photograph showing Nazi officials in military uniforms examining documents, maps, or plans on a table. Multiple figures are bent over studying the materials intently.",
                        "keyfact": "The Nazis held looted art in salt mines at Altaussee, such as the Early Netherlandish Ghent Altarpiece, which sustained significant damage."
                      },
                      {
                        "image_summary": "A color photograph of a dramatic Alpine landscape featuring a silhouetted figure standing on a rocky mountain peak. The scene shows multiple mountain peaks shrouded in clouds and mist, creating a romantic and imposing atmosphere.",
                        "keyfact": "The image is credited to Caspar David Friedrich and depicts the romantic Alpine scenery that fascinated Hitler throughout his life."
                      }
                    ]
                  },
